we're traveling along the indus highway  in sindh province between seven sharif  and dadu where we've been reporting from  over the last few days and uh  all this water here this is not a  natural river or a canal this is rain  water from this so-called monster  monsoon it has completely besieged this  area all the homes have water going into  them and the electricity pylons you can  see behind me they're completely  submerged there's no electricity in many  parts pakistan already struggles with  electricity  service and there's no um  telephone signal a lot of lines are down  and internet is wishful thinking in many  parts but beyond that it's the basics  they don't have food  and water and shelter the people here  from the this area they've many of them  have moved out of their own homes and  into makeshift tents some of them  kept back from other  disasters back in 2010 when they had  floods in this area others have used a  piece of clothing and sticks to make  their tents they don't want to leave  their homes they don't want to go too  far into camps or or move along like  we've seen in the last few days other  people have moved to other areas where  they can fly dry find dry land but they  want to stay close to their homes  they're hoping the water will recede  but that also means while they stay  close to this water they are washing in  it they are drinking from it and of  course  there are no bathrooms around here this  is sewage water mixed with this monsoon  water and there is a serious concern for  water-borne diseases
 

